# SRG-APP-000295-AU-000190  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must automatically terminate a user session after
# organization-defined conditions or trigger events requiring session
# disconnect.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000295-AU-000190
group:           V-221922
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-002361
control_rev5:    AC-12

contribution:    Splunk terminates sessions on its own timer: web.conf
                 tools.sessions.timeout expires the Splunk Web session after a set period
                 of inactivity, and ui_inactivity_timeout must not be set below 1, which
                 would stop the browser-side pollers ever yielding and keep the session
                 alive indefinitely.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout present
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout max 5
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout min 2
check:           web.conf [settings] ui_inactivity_timeout min 1
check:           web.conf [settings] ui_inactivity_timeout max 10
check:           server.conf [general] sessionTimeout matches ^(([1-9]|1[0-5])m|([1-9]|[1-9][0-9]|[1-8][0-9]{2}|900)s)$
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/manage-users/configure-user-session-timeouts
vendor:          web.conf [settings] ui_inactivity_timeout https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/manage-users/configure-user-session-timeouts
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The organization defines the conditions and trigger events beyond
                 inactivity that require session disconnect - incident response actions,
                 time-of-day restrictions - and Splunk expresses only the time-based
                 trigger.
external:        The product STIG scopes SPLK-CL-000010 to the search head, so on an
                 instance that is not a search head the benchmark authority declares the
                 rule Not Applicable.
external:        Where a reverse proxy, load balancer or SSO gateway fronts Splunk Web, its
                 own session timer also has to expire, and it is not an application
                 setting.
external:        Terminating the operating system session or the remote-access session that
                 carries the browser is outside the application.

citation:        Three of the reviewed conditions fail a configuration built exactly as the
                 benchmark authority prescribes. The product STIG fix for SPLK-CL-000010
                 sets one key, tools.session.timeout = 15, and its check makes a finding
                 only where tools.sessions.timeout is missing or 16 or more.
                 ui_inactivity_timeout max 15 appears in no check or fix text for that key,
                 and its documented default is 60 (web.conf.spec:403-410), so the condition
                 reports a finding against an instance configured per the fix text
                 verbatim, which is a false finding of the most damaging kind.
                 ui_inactivity_timeout present fails for the same reason: the default of 60
                 is at or above 1 and does not defeat termination, so demanding the key be
                 declared penalizes a compliant default. server.conf [general]
                 sessionTimeout present is also dropped here: the setting defaults to "1"
                 (1 hour) and therefore already terminates the splunkd session
                 (server.conf.spec:73-78), so requiring its declaration again fails the
                 STIG-prescribed configuration; the eight-hour bound on that key is tested
                 where the check text supplies a number, in SRG-APP-000295-WSR-000012. What
                 is kept is grounded and directional: max 15 matches the product STIG check
                 ("16 or more" is a finding), min 2 is the specification's own floor ("Use
                 a value of 2 or higher, as a value of 1 causes a race condition with the
                 browser refresh", web.conf.spec:924-932), and ui_inactivity_timeout min 1
                 catches the one value a site can set that defeats the requirement
                 outright, since below 1 "Sessions stay alive for as long as the browser is
                 open" (web.conf.spec:403-410); an absent key resolves to the documented
                 default of 60 and satisfies it.
finding:         Splunk Web does not expire inactive sessions within the required period:
                 web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout is unset or 16 or more minutes,
                 or ui_inactivity_timeout is set below 1, which stops the inactivity
                 notification firing and keeps sessions alive for as long as the browser
                 stays open.
